WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.880
- [Narrator] When Fed
Chair Jerome Powell speaks.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.610
- Today, the FOMC raised
its policy interest rate

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:07.170
by a quarter percentage point.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.540
- [Narrator] The markets move.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.240
That's because the head
of the US Federal Reserve

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.420
is the most powerful
economic voice in the nation,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.350
and Fed chairs can generally
be put into two categories.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.710
- Hawks are more worried
about controlling inflation

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.720
and doves tend to be more worried

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.690
about making sure the
employment market's strong.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.330
- [Narrator] But Powell
isn't one or the other.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.910
Here's how Powell's
mindset helped him rise

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.703
to the top and keep the Fed united.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.440
The US Federal Reserve
functions as a bank for banks.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.210
- We look after the overall stability

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:44.700
of the financial system and
we have tools to do that.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.990
- Recently most Fed chairs have
been academics or economists

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.940
so he's a little bit different

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.880
from the Fed chairs that we've
had in the past few decades.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.700
- He was never trained in
formal economic modeling

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.540
but he's smart and he's got a
great staff and he catches on.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.490
- [Narrator] After a career
in investment banking

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:03.870
and law in New York,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.350
Powell left to become an
undersecretary at the Treasury.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.140
- Speaking for myself, that
accounted in large measure

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.470
for my own conduct at the meeting,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.910
which was to be fairly passive.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.340
- [Narrator] In 2012, he
joined the Fed's Board

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.590
of Governors to help
guide the organization.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.880
- He has a fine reputation.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.310
He should be a fine member.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.750
- [Narrator] But his practical experience

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:27.480
in finance gave him a different skillset.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.090
- Powell hasn't been
preparing his whole life

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.440
to be Fed chair.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.610
He hasn't been part

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.450
of this priesthood of
monetary policy theorists

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.310
and so that's brought different strengths

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.860
and weaknesses for him in the job.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:41.940
- He watches the data.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.150
He listens to what
economists and modelers say

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:47.940
and what financial
market people are saying

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.070
but not what politicians are saying.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.840
- [Narrator] His prioritization of data,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.850
not economic models has meant

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.800
that he's not always a hawk,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.210
economic shorthand for those

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.430
who prioritize fighting inflation,

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.230
and he's not always a dove,

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.360
taking action to boost employment.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.400
- He's a pragmatist. He's a realist.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.480
He's not an ideologue, and so
he's going to change his mind

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.510
when he sees the facts
on the ground changing.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.570
- [Narrator] And things
certainly changed in March 2020.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.270
- [Reporter 1] The toll
is growing by the hour.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:20.520
- [Reporter 2] Major
retailers are also going dark.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:21.510
- It doesn't seem fair

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.940
that people should lose
everything they have,

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.740
including their homes over this.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.570
- [Narrator] Powell's
data-driven mentality

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.510
was guiding the largest
economic stimulus plan

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.460
ever undertaken in the US.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.890
- At that point, the whole
Federal Reserve board,

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:36.660
certainly including Jay Powell

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.020
are leaning very strongly
in the dovish direction.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.780
- The Fed can also
contribute in important ways

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.000
by providing a measure
of relief and stability

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.580
during this period of
constrained economic activity

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.170
and by using our tools to ensure

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.620
that the eventual recovery
is as vigorous as possible.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.270
- [Narrator] In his
response to the emergency,

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.370
Powell stretched his power farther

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.530
than Fed chairs had done before.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.637
- He got after his staff, he said,

00:03:00.637 --> 00:03:01.980
"We're falling behind this thing."

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.380
He said, "It feels like we're
swimming after a speedboat."

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.450
He encouraged them to
really get on top of this

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.580
and they just announced a bunch of stuff

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.770
this one weekend of March 2020.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.960
- [Narrator] He instructed
the Fed to begin a torrent

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.720
of bond-buying programs
to stabilize markets

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.970
and slash interest rates.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.940
That month, it bought Treasury
and mortgage securities

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.150
at a pace of nearly $75 billion a day.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.198
- The criticism on Powell has been

00:03:26.198 --> 00:03:29.670
that maybe he overreacts to
the problem at the moment,

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.310
so you kept interest rates too low

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.860
when maybe you should
have been more concerned

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.920
about the risk of an inflation shock.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.350
- [Narrator] That risk became a reality.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.200
Fed holdings had reached $9 trillion

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.900
and inflation had hit a 40-year high.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.140
- As Americans well know
it remains painfully high.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.060
- [Narrator] Powell had to pivot again

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.850
and tried to keep the Fed's
rate-setting committee

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:58.230
or FOMC voting together
to get policy passed.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.350
He began acting as a hawk
to bring down inflation.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.350
- But everything that
economists thought they knew

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.980
about inflation in 2021
turned out to be pretty wrong.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.740
- It's not going to be,
we don't think smooth.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.090
It's probably gonna be bumpy.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.350
- I think at a time like this,

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.270
we're very fortunate to have somebody

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.900
with the temperament of Jay
Powell, who listens to people,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.150
understands different points of view,

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.260
distills the different pieces
of information that come in

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.800
to the Fed and makes considered
judgements about what to do

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:32.550
and then goes and persuades
people that maybe on one extreme

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.740
or the other extreme to come

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.630
into the center and go
along with the consensus.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:41.910
- [Narrator] Powell kept
the hawks and doves united

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.530
with only one dissenting vote

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.800
against rate raises since the end of 2021.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.190
- A lot of the people I talked
to say that he's very good

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.950
at taking people's concerns seriously,

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.320
making them feel like
they're being listened to,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.730
figuring out what a reasonable
compromise position might be

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:02.010
and that I think explains
why he's been able

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:03.660
to keep the committee so united

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.240
during this pretty difficult stretch.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.850
- Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.830
We at the Fed will do everything we can

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.280
to achieve our maximum employment
and price stability goals.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.113
- [Narrator] As he confronts

00:05:15.113 --> 00:05:17.370
an uncertain economic landscape,

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.790
his empirical mindset will
continue to be tested.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.100
- How we look back on Powell's tenure

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.740
as chair really depends on what happens

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.890
with inflation and the economy
over the next two years.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:33.890
- How well did he handle
this uncertain period?

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:38.700
What did the economy look
like in 2024 and 2025?

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.450
That history is yet to be written

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.190
and I think future historians

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.395
of the Fed will judge him
strongly on how that looks.

00:05:48.395 --> 00:05:51.062
(pensive music)

